# Architecture

`docs/ARCHITECTURE.md` is the source of truth. Summary:

- Layers: Core, Scene, Effects, Ui. Dependencies point downward, and Scene holds one thing
  of the Effects: what they own on a material (`LeafMaterial.Copy`, `FallingMaterial`,
  `Emitter`, fields those effects set and clear). `Lifecycle` (Core) is the one class that
  calls Scene and Effects. Effects never reference each other: Falling
  Leaves gets Wind's values as arguments from `Lifecycle`.
- Ownership: `LeafMaterial` owns the renderer slots and the restore; `ShadowFix` owns the
  copy; `FallingLeaves` owns the emitter, the leaf material and the sprite jobs (one per
  source texture, shared); `LeafShader` owns the bundle. `TreeRegistry` calls each effect's
  `Release(LeafMaterial)` before a tree is released.
- Frame order in `Lifecycle.Update`: every frame compare the effects' switches and sliders
  with the values last applied (a dozen comparisons, no allocation; their storables carry
  no callbacks) and check the leaf shader reference; every `SceneScanSeconds` scan the
  trees, read MSAA, retry stalled leaf-shape jobs (`FallingLeaves.Tick`); then for each
  dirty tree gather it first when its renderers are stale (`Tree.NeedsGather`), then
  `ShadowFix.ApplyTree`, `Wind.Apply` per material, `FallingLeaves.ApplyTree`. Each step
  in `log.RunOnce`, timed. Once every tree is applied a frame does the comparisons and
  nothing else.
- Patterns: Facade, RAII ownership, Observer via dirty flags, Command for Reset and Copy.
  No DI container, no event bus, no reflection wiring.
- Every storable is registered through `ParamSet` with a feature id, a scope (Look, Scene,
  Ui, Volatile, plus the Private flag), and a stable key. The scene carries Look, Scene and
  Ui in one block beside the `trees` node; `Settings` migrates by schema number (renames
  and rewrites only).
- Save keys are the schema and never change. Storable names are `<Feature> <Param>
  <unit>` and may change (Timeline references break, saves do not).
